20
00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.630
Justin Traglia: Yep, thanks. So, the solutions are from…

21
00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:43.409
Justin Traglia: Potuz and I, and from Nico, the PR that I submitted

22
00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:53.189
Justin Traglia: Pretty much tells the proposer that they should accept bids, or duplicate bids from the same builder, regardless of value.

23
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.790
Justin Traglia: But only as the proposer.

24
00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:04.059
Justin Traglia: Nico can go into… is PR, but essentially it allows builders to submit increasingly valuable bids.

25
00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.489
Justin Traglia: Above a certain threshold.

26
00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:14.819
Justin Traglia: I actually prefer Nico's PR, but I'm curious to hear what clients think. Nico, would you like to go into more detail?

27
00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:22.660
Nico Flaig: Yeah, so it solves the same problem, right? The proposer would accept multiple bits.

28
00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:32.989
Nico Flaig: But it basically also makes every other node in the network relay multiple bits of a builder, but at, like, a minimum increment, so…

29
00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:38.410
Nico Flaig: The minimum increment is mostly to, prevent DOS, and,

30
00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:48.530
Nico Flaig: Like, the highest bid rule is still, kept in place that we have, so if there's already a higher bid, then nodes will also ignore bids from a builder.

31
00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:53.350
Nico Flaig: Yeah, there's, like, the formula, it's in the PR.

32
00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:59.510
Nico Flaig: So it's like a 3% increment per bit that you need to do as a builder.

33
00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:04.930
Nico Flaig: And a minimum of, like, 0.001 EF.

34
00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:10.029
Nico Flaig: That a builder needs to bid up, in order for the bid to be propagated.

35
00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:16.139
Nico Flaig: The good or the nice side effect for this is that it also makes honest bidding a lot better.

36
00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:26.939
Nico Flaig: Compared to the current spec, because right now builders need to set up multiple indices to do stage bidding on P2P, and with that, you can just have a single index.

37
00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:31.790
Nico Flaig: Where you have all your liquidity, and you don't need to split it across multiple indices.

38
00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:40.109
Nico Flaig: I guess the downside is it allows for more bids to be submitted on P2P, but…

39
00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:44.940
Nico Flaig: It's quite costly because, to increase the bid value, you actually

40
00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:51.210
Nico Flaig: Need to increment it by 3%, and if the bid wins, you need to pay that to the proposal.

41
00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.210
Nico Flaig: So that is, like, the spam protection it adds.

42
00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:01.309
Justin Traglia: And you're still competing with other builders as well. Like, I did the math earlier this morning, and…

43
00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:10.770
Justin Traglia: If you wanted… if a builder were to submit a bid up to half an ETH, that would limit it to, like, 200 bids per slot.

44
00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:17.239
Justin Traglia: And then if… If its limit were, .05 ETH, that would be 125 bits.

45
00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.449
Justin Traglia: So it's very reasonable still.

46
00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.079
Justin Traglia: And bids aren't super large.

47
00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:29.080
stokes: Okay, yeah, thanks for the overview.

48
00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.779
stokes: Any client teams had a chance to look at this?

49
00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:47.570
stokes: Okay.

50
00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:55.190
Barnabas: Are we comfortable with the 3%, by the way? Just because it's like a weird number. I'm not sure where it's coming from.

51
00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:05.779
Nico Flaig: So that was what I had for a while in Lodestar, but we can, like, just analyze more Manu data to tweak this.

52
00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:16.779
Nico Flaig: It's like the cost you need to, like, at the lower you set it, the more competitive you make the auction, P2P, but the higher…

53
00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:21.040
Nico Flaig: Said it, more costly, so…

54
00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.269
Nico Flaig: Just needs to be some better…

55
00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:33.450
Barnabas: Could this not be just a client implementation detail? Like, why do we have to output this in the spec?

56
00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:39.840
Justin Traglia: So it's, it's useful for builders to have a consistent

57
00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:43.030
Justin Traglia: implementation of this. Otherwise, they would just,

58
00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:52.510
Justin Traglia: go… or sorry, they'd submit bids for the lowest denominator, so if, like, one client implemented 1% and the rest implemented 3%,

59
00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:57.460
Justin Traglia: They don't know who the proposer is, so they would increment by… 1%.

60
00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:02.379
Justin Traglia: Or they'll do the highest, I forget, but it's just inconsistent and complicated for them.

61
00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:06.910
stokes: Yeah, Potuz?

62
00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:23.189
Potuz: I just want to express my dis… my discomfort with this kind of EIPs. We are already over-complicating and over-specifying this topic, which is a global topic. It's going to float… I'm going to be able to flute the chain. I understand that

63
00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:40.209
Potuz: There are ways of preventing DOS by adding this minimum increase. However, we are already doing crazy things, like allowing for bits to propagate on their non-finalization, coming from different forks, and we've already seen bugs in our caches.

64
00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:47.620
Potuz: dealing with these dependent routes, because we decided to go with this design of allowing a lot of bits over P2P.

65
00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:53.709
Potuz: I think we should keep this topic as simple as possible. What we have today, it's fine.

66
00:11:53.710 --> 00:12:09.010
Potuz: The bug that this is… that triggered all of this discussion is a crazy bug in which a builder sends out, for everyone a very high bid and directly connects to a proposer and sends a very low-valuable bid.

67
00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:18.109
Potuz: It's completely solved at the proposer side. There's no rational reason why a proposer would want to ignore any bid, so…

68
00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:29.449
Potuz: Even… even… it should be fine, even if we implement Nico's PR. The proposer should not ignore any bit that comes to it anyways, even with lower values.

69
00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:33.370
Potuz: Because I am the proposer, I can just put whatever bid I want.

70
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:41.190
Potuz: So anyways, we're going to be implementing a thing that is… the proposer doesn't ignore anything, and I would want to prevent this…

71
00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.469
Potuz: topic to taking over P2P.

72
00:12:43.750 --> 00:13:02.949
Potuz: I don't see any reason why we would increase complexity over this, and I would anyways try to prevent this on Prysm. I would feel much more comfortable just accepting one bid than having to have a cache and checking from each builder what was the latest increase, or what is the latest increase that I have.

73
00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:05.210
Potuz: And add computations to this topic.

74
00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:06.899
Potuz: It just doesn't make any sense to me.

75
00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:12.970
stokes: Okay. That's a fair point.

