634
01:16:35.600 --> 01:16:46.590
Dima Gusakov | Lido: From hard debates to a very fun, interesting, and great EIP. Okay, 8148, custom sleep threshold for Validators, and why do we even need it in Habitat?

635
01:16:46.920 --> 01:16:49.330
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Let's start with the basics, what's the problem?

636
01:16:49.630 --> 01:17:04.100
Dima Gusakov | Lido: So what we have today, except on top of this wonderful animation that I personally absolutely love, is we have type 1 validators, where you have 32 ETH, any rewards above, automatically transferred to withdrawable wallet.

637
01:17:04.110 --> 01:17:11.780
Dima Gusakov | Lido: User experience that people used to over the ages, well, actually over the… 3 years or something.

638
01:17:11.990 --> 01:17:14.100
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And then we have type 2 validators.

639
01:17:14.650 --> 01:17:17.900
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Exactly the same, but with 2048E.

640
01:17:18.890 --> 01:17:26.159
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And… yes, you can move, some stake away from Type 2 Validator, because the…

641
01:17:26.510 --> 01:17:37.429
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Minimal activation balance of 32 EF, but to do that, you need to do it manually. You need to do a partial withdrawal, which is a subject of an exit queue, you need to do a transaction, and so on and so forth.

642
01:17:37.430 --> 01:17:46.760
Dima Gusakov | Lido: So, if we speak primarily about this amazing experience illustrated by these animations, we have a choice of 32 or 2048 EF.

643
01:17:47.470 --> 01:17:59.660
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And that's the core of the problem. Why? Because, well, not that many people have 2048 ETH, and we believe this stops consolidations a lot to what we are proposing.

644
01:17:59.840 --> 01:18:08.889
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Well, we're proposing to make The size of the Validator custom, or set a custom sweep threshold.

645
01:18:09.110 --> 01:18:10.740
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And not just custom.

646
01:18:10.950 --> 01:18:19.980
Dima Gusakov | Lido: But also configurable, so that after you create your customly sized Validator, you can change the size of this validator moving forward.

647
01:18:20.310 --> 01:18:26.210
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Using execution layer contract, somewhat similar to what we have in 7.002, for example.

648
01:18:28.010 --> 01:18:36.390
Dima Gusakov | Lido: So, with EIP8148, there will be no more choice between 32 and 2048 ETH.

649
01:18:36.600 --> 01:18:37.710
Dima Gusakov | Lido: there will be…

650
01:18:37.820 --> 01:18:51.709
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Anything in between. Starting from 32, ending up at 2048 ETH with one ETH stack. And again, you can set it when you create your Validator, you can configure it later, so you can raise it, you can lower it.

651
01:18:51.940 --> 01:18:56.100
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Doing a partial withdrawal before that, and so on.

652
01:18:56.640 --> 01:18:59.990
Dima Gusakov | Lido: So let's talk explicitly about the benefits of the CAP.

653
01:19:00.220 --> 01:19:14.299
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Well, first one is that with this EIP, we will finally have just one type of withdrawal credentials that fits them all. We've already discussed, previously on the call, deprecation of Type 0.

654
01:19:14.520 --> 01:19:18.269
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Validators. But we still have type 1 and type 2, which…

655
01:19:18.800 --> 01:19:32.930
Dima Gusakov | Lido: It's not the best situation in the world. With this EIP, you will only have type 2 that will fit them all, because you can have still… you still can have 32 EF validators if you wish, you can still have 2048 EF validators, and any size of the validator in between.

656
01:19:33.610 --> 01:19:46.479
Dima Gusakov | Lido: We believe that with this EIP, we will get faster and more consolidations. As you can see right now from the consolidation queue, the only major spike and the only actual interest in consolidation was from Lido.

657
01:19:46.480 --> 01:19:54.699
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And currently, our consolidations are unstop, not because of that reason, but still. Without Lido, no one else is basically consolidating.

658
01:19:54.730 --> 01:20:13.080
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And the majority of the people I talked to indicated that people, I mean, protocols, people, professional operators, indicated that this liquidity concern, this… them losing this ability to get rewards automatically, is the deal-breaker for them to go and consolidate their validators.

659
01:20:13.330 --> 01:20:21.699
Dima Gusakov | Lido: There is a potential to raise MaxCB, because this 2048 ETH limit in the current EIP8148 implementation

660
01:20:21.900 --> 01:20:41.560
Dima Gusakov | Lido: can easily be raised without introducing a new withdrawal credential type, without migrating any validators, and every other validator already existing on Ethereum would keep their custom sweep threshold value, so their size, and later on, they will be able to opt-in and decide whether they want to go bigger or not.

661
01:20:42.410 --> 01:20:51.710
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And there will definitely be a reduced withdrawal queue pressure, because most of this day-by-day withdrawals will be now served with sweep withdrawals.

662
01:20:52.410 --> 01:20:55.819
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Now let's talk about expected effects of this EIP.

663
01:20:55.890 --> 01:21:10.319
Dima Gusakov | Lido: First of all, we expect, realistically, from 3% to 10% more of Ethereum state being consolidated in the near term, should this EIP be implemented. This is a rough estimation based on internal demand within Lido.

664
01:21:10.320 --> 01:21:20.389
Dima Gusakov | Lido: from CSM stakers, and conversations with multiple professional operators and non-custodial staking operators who are still using Type 1

665
01:21:20.460 --> 01:21:24.659
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Withdrawal credentials, specifically because of this liquidity concerns.

666
01:21:24.980 --> 01:21:43.189
Dima Gusakov | Lido: There is an immediate improvement in UX for the new validators, because EIP8148 allows you to set the size of your validator when you are creating it. So if you are starting with 256 ETH, for example, or even more weird number, like 2050,

667
01:21:43.420 --> 01:21:58.249
Dima Gusakov | Lido: You can now set the size of your Validator precisely to this value and enjoy your sweep rewards from, I don't know, next week or something like that. And there is no more dilemma for you, either to go with Type 2 or Type 1. You go straight to Type 2.

668
01:21:58.940 --> 01:22:09.609
Dima Gusakov | Lido: There is a real potential to deprecate Type 1 validators in the future, should this EIP be implemented, because again, there will be no need for Type 1 validators anymore.

669
01:22:10.220 --> 01:22:13.160
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And we'll have hundreds of happy zkers, for sure.

670
01:22:14.970 --> 01:22:19.630
Dima Gusakov | Lido: You might ask me what's the demand for this EIP, and am I just talking here because…

671
01:22:19.630 --> 01:22:22.240
stokes: Just a time check, get the speed up the slide.

672
01:22:22.240 --> 01:22:31.629
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, it's just… it's just two more minutes. So, there is definitely a huge demand, both from staking protocols, solo stakers, and professional operators.

673
01:22:32.850 --> 01:22:44.500
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Briefly on what's already done for this EIP. We have Consensus specs that are already merged into feature folder. We have spec tests that are waiting to be merged should this EIP be CFI'd.

674
01:22:44.500 --> 01:22:55.449
Dima Gusakov | Lido: We have system contract code ready, and the system contract itself is not that much different from the existing system contracts that we are using for 7002 or 7251, and Lido will be happy to pay for the audit.

675
01:22:55.730 --> 01:23:12.630
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And we have a working proof of concept for Prysm, plus Kath, plus even DORA, all thanks to Manu, so this EIP is working, and we have a real estimation on how hard it is to implement it, and spoiler alert, it's very simple.

676
01:23:13.480 --> 01:23:15.109
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Now to the final notes.

677
01:23:15.440 --> 01:23:25.150
Dima Gusakov | Lido: This is a future-proof, small additive change, so not a deal-breaker, not a consensus-breaking change, just a small thing, easy to implement, but really adds value.

678
01:23:25.510 --> 01:23:37.429
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Independent security researchers reviewed and approved this EIP so that they haven't found any major issues to the Ethereum should this EIP be implemented, and they genuinely think that this is a valuable thing.

679
01:23:37.900 --> 01:23:44.679
Dima Gusakov | Lido: current state with two types of withdrawal credentials is misleading, and I bet someone else, someone will argue with me on that.

680
01:23:45.110 --> 01:23:58.749
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And Hegota is the best time to have this EIP, because moving forward, you guys will have a lot of work with PQ stuff, and so on and so forth. So, given that the CIP is pretty simple, and Hegota is mostly EL heavy rather than CL-heavy.

681
01:23:58.910 --> 01:24:02.939
Dima Gusakov | Lido: I definitely think we should consider it for implementing in Hegota.

682
01:24:03.190 --> 01:24:18.940
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Final note for those who are very concerned about potential state bloat because of this EIP, there is a potential to change specs a little bit, and use unused bytes in withdrawal credentials, not just for the initial sweep value, but also for the future changes. It's just…

683
01:24:19.460 --> 01:24:22.670
Dima Gusakov | Lido: thinking… Thinking about the questions you might ask.

